when the coronavirus first arrived in  the united states  few places were hit as hard as nursing  homes and few nursing homes were hit as  hard as the menlo park veterans memorial  home  in central new jersey a wall street  journal investigation found that nearly  100 people died at menlo in april  more than 10 times the number in a  typical month  [Music]  a spokesman for the state agency that  runs menlo declined to respond to most  of the journal's questions  citing expected litigation but in august  reporter chris weaver  interviewed vietnam veteran and  surviving resident glenn osborne  to understand more about what happened  to the residents of menlo park  now that we're nearly six months out  from the beginnings of  the pandemic do you feel like you know  the status of all the residents who you  knew before the outbreak began  i would say 85 i know there's a few i  don't know  and i would like to know of a couple  residents that i knew  socially where they are  recently even though i'm not supposed  to wander out i've gone out and i would  find them  they're so weak they can hardly talk  and they're so pale and they're not  moving and they're still trying to  recover from this disease  it's a horrible thing to see among the  dead  were 84 year old isabella kovacs and 86  year old joan williams  their stories provide a window into what  went wrong at menlo park  [Music]  the menlo park memorial veterans home  accepts veterans their spouses and gold  star parents  it has 312 beds in six residential units  all with patriotic names stars and  stripes where the dementia patients  lived  and freedom where joan lived  joan moved to menlo in 2015 she was  elected to be representative to the  resident council  for about three years running she would  travel  up and down the hallway of freedom  talking to the residents asking what is  their concerns  and that can go on for a half hour at a  time sometimes  we would go to visit her and even  family members of other residents knew  my mother on a first name basis  she always had her makeup together her  hair done and  you know just a classy type of lady  isabella kovacs arrived at menlo park in  january 2020  she had served in both the army and the  air force like joan she was in the  freedom unit  once we got here they had a welcome a  little room  the big sign that said welcome on a  chalkboard and they had a balloon for  her  and they brought us all beverages and  offered us some lunch  but she would have little time to settle  in  not long after isabella arrived there  was an outbreak in the life care nursing  home in kirkland washington  and soon after the coronavirus began  spreading rapidly in nursing homes  around the country  still menlo was caught flat-footed by  the outbreak and the administration's  best attempts to mitigate the spread  fell  short they created a quarantine area for  coveted patients within the alzheimer's  unit  stars and stripes it was separated from  the rest of the unit with a plastic tarp  and the home resisted other more obvious  safety measures  one of the things that we keep hearing  about is that the staff  weren't wearing protective equipment all  the time and in fact that it sounds like  the administration might have instructed  them not to  for some reason yes i actually witnessed  senior administration physically removed  the protective equipment from the  storage cabinet  they were said we don't want you to wear  it because you'll frighten the residents  and that frightened me more than  anything staff members claim they were  not allowed to wear masks  outside the quarantine unit a march 27  email from the new jersey department of  veterans affairs to menlo ceo  outlined the escalating consequences for  mask insubordination  oral counseling written warning  official reprimand and suspension  menlo spokesman declined to comment but  brigadier general mark patierski  who is deputy director of the agency at  the time but has since retired  said masks were in short supply and the  agency  was following federal guidelines  joan and isabella's story takes a turn  in the end of march  i spoke to her on a regular basis i  spoke to the nurse supervisor  she was doing fine she was doing fine  on march 30th menlo resident rose dente  tested positive for the virus  at an emergency room at a nearby  hospital  she died the next day the virus was  inside menlo park but the administration  didn't tell residents families april 3rd  is when i lost communication with my mom  the fourth  still can't get in touch with mom the  fifth  can't get in touch with the nurses  station can't get in touch with mom  joan was somewhere on the freedom unit a  few days after sherry lost track of her  she was put into room 239  alongside isabella kovacs it was obvious  by then joan was very sick  she was asleep all the time and they  were in there checking her temperature  all day long she said  the staff wouldn't tell me anything but  in an april 6  email the home ceo elizabeth schiff  heedles asked for permission to tell  residents and their family members that  kovid was spreading  the next day april 7th she mailed that  letter  but more than a week after the first  resident tested positive for coronavirus  it was too late for joan she was  transferred to room 753  in stars and stripes and later that  night to the hospital  mom tested positive for covid  her lungs were in very bad condition so  it was like a perfect  storm back at menlo isabella was getting  sick  she too was transferred somewhere in  stars and stripes i asked three  different staff members  if they were going to test her for coven  and all three said we don't test for  coping here  on april 12th well into the outbreak of  menlo  the new jersey department of military  and veterans affairs  posted this video on facebook it shows  the staff and ceo of the home  shift heedles dancing in the hallways in  an apparent effort  to raise the resident's spirits menlo  declined to comment  they trying to say they're trying to  cheer us up  i was in shock they were dancing in the  hallways  where literally the residents were dying  in the rooms that they were passing by  the plastic sheet separating stars and  stripes from the rest of the facility  was sagging  staff members in full ppe went back and  forth across the partition  more and more residents were being  shuffled throughout the home  isabella was sent to the emergency room  on april 17th  and also tested positive for covin she  and joan were dying  they let me going up i put on two masks  and a face shield i had two pairs of  gloves  i told her about heaven and i listed the  names of  everybody she's gonna see again  i told her that she was the best mother  i've ever could have had and how much i  loved her  and they're actually gonna get this mask  off and she's gonna  fly free and  i couldn't i couldn't stay in there that  long because i was just  i didn't want her to just hear me cry i  was just crying a lot so  and then the nurse knocked on the door  and i told her i said mom i love you  and that's the less  that i was with my mother  unfortunately both passed away within a  couple of days of one another  i wanted to you know share those details  with you only because it does seem like  you know her particular past kind of  covers some of the same ground that we  were speaking about generally earlier  means a lot to me i appreciate it thank  you  joan williams died on april 21st  isabella kovacs died on april 23rd  their families are among those that have  begun the process of suing the agency  that runs menlo  the agency reported just 62 deaths in  its official tally  a spokesman said that count is based on  expert's conclusions  and that the agency mourns the loss of  every veteran at its facilities  but dozens more died with coveted-like  symptoms according to internal patient  records  many of those residents were never  tested their families say  the new jersey department of health  ultimately confirmed the journal's  findings  revealing another 39 probable coveted  deaths at menlo  i have lost more friends more good  veterans than i did in the war and  combat  these are true heroes that they fought  for america  they were wounded soldiers and came back  came back from world war ii in korea and  vietnam  only to be left here  and treated within dignity and not cared  for not protected  [Music]  you
 

